      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the perimeter of the larger triangle, we need to understand the concept of similar triangles. When two triangles are similar, the ratios of the corresponding sides are equal. In this case, the ratio of the longest side of the smaller triangle to the longest side of the larger triangle is equal to the ratio of the perimeter of the smaller triangle to the perimeter of the larger triangle.\n\nGiven:\n- The side lengths of the smaller isosceles triangle are 8 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm.\n- The longest side of the larger triangle is 25 cm.\n\nThe ratio of the longest side of the smaller triangle to the longest side of the larger triangle is:\n\\[ \\frac{10}{25} = \\frac{2}{5} \\]\n\nLet \\( P_s \\) be the perimeter of the smaller triangle and \\( P_l \\) be the perimeter of the larger triangle. The ratio of the perimeters is the same as the ratio of the corresponding sides:\n\\[ \\frac{P_s}{P_l} = \\frac{2}{5} \\]\n\nWe know that the side lengths of the smaller triangle are 8 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm, so the perimeter \\( P_s \\) is:\n\\[ P_s = 8 + 8 + 10 = 26 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nNow, we can find \\( P_l \\) using the ratio:\n\\[ P_l = \\frac{5}{2} \\times P_s = \\frac{5}{2} \\times 26 = 65 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of the larger triangle is:\n\\[ \\boxed{65} \\]",
